{"name":"Thistle Green","hex":"#cccaa8","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"thistle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several perennial composite plants, especially of genera Cirsium, Carduus, Cynara, or Onopordum, having prickly leaves and showy flower heads with prickly bracts.","This plant seen as the national emblem of Scotland."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thistle"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thistle","blocks":[{"text":"Thistle is the popular name of a group of flowering plants characterised by leaves with sharp spikes on the margins, mostly in the family Asteraceae. Prickles can also occur all over the plant – on the stem, and on the flat parts of the leaves. These prickles protect the plant from herbivores. Typically, an involucre with a clasping shape, similar to a cup or urn, subtends each of a thistle's flower heads. The typically feathery pappus of a ripe thistle flower is known as thistle-down."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Carduus_Nutans_in_the_Carpathian_Mountains.jpg/330px-Carduus_Nutans_in_the_Carpathian_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thistle"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"},{"title":"Shades of purple","blocks":[{"text":"There are numerous variations of the color purple, a sampling of which is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Shades_of_Purple.svg/330px-Shades_of_Purple.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_purple"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Milk_thistle_flowerhead.jpg/500px-Milk_thistle_flowerhead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Milk_thistle_flowerhead.jpg/1280px-Milk_thistle_flowerhead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Milk_thistle_flowerhead.jpg/500px-Milk_thistle_flowerhead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Milk thistle flowerhead","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMilk_thistle_flowerhead.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Red_Thistle.JPG/500px-Red_Thistle.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Red_Thistle.JPG/1280px-Red_Thistle.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Red_Thistle.JPG/500px-Red_Thistle.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cirsium arizonicum, showing arachnoid cobwebbiness on stems and leaves, with ants attending aphids that might be taking advantage of the shelter.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARed_Thistle.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Spear_Thistle%2C_Scotland_1.jpg/500px-Spear_Thistle%2C_Scotland_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Spear_Thistle%2C_Scotland_1.jpg/500px-Spear_Thistle%2C_Scotland_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spear Thistle Cirsium vulgare in Scotland, 2007","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpear_Thistle%2C_Scotland_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Musk_Thistle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_893820.jpg/500px-Musk_Thistle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_893820.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Musk_Thistle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_893820.jpg/500px-Musk_Thistle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_893820.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carduus nutans in the early morning light.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMusk_Thistle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_893820.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Thistledown_1781.JPG/500px-Thistledown_1781.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c